> Note: this is a re-report of #2597, which was closed for not following the bug bounty template. The bug itself was not evaluated. This report follows the template and reproduces the bug live through the public `findAssetInfo` function. The examples in the closed #2597 were wrong (ETH is not at X1 Parachain 2006 on AssetHubPolkadot, and DOT on Polkadot has `parents: 1`, not `parents: 0`); the examples below were re-verified against the real asset registry.

# Bug Report

## Describe the Bug

`findAssetInfoByLoc` treats the same location differently depending on whether it arrives as an object or as a string. The object branch canonicalizes before comparing (V3 `X1`-object to V5 `X1`-array, `'Here'` to `{ Here: null }`, key order and bigint differences ignored). The string branch does none of that: it compares the raw string against `JSON.stringify(asset.location)`.

File: `packages/assets/src/assets/search/findAssetInfoByLoc.ts`

```ts
if (typeof location === 'string') {
  return foreignAssets.find(asset => compareLocations(location, asset)) // raw string compare
}

const normalizedLocation = canonicalizeLocation(location)
return foreignAssets.find(
  asset => asset.location && deepEqual(normalizedLocation, canonicalizeLocation(asset.location))
)
```

A string location is a first-class public input: `TLocationValue = string | TLocation`, so `Builder().currency({ location: '...' })`, `findAssetInfo`, `findAssetInfoOnDest`, and swap asset resolution all accept it. It is the natural form for JSON configs, API responses, and serialized registry data. The same location that resolves as an object silently returns `null` as a string, with no error and no way to distinguish "asset does not exist" from "same location, serialized differently".

## To Reproduce

```ts
import { findAssetInfo } from '@paraspell/assets' // version 14.5.3

// 1. V3 X1-object form — UNQ on Acala, stored location
//    { parents: 1, interior: { X1: [{ Parachain: 2037 }] } }
findAssetInfo('Acala', { location: { parents: 1, interior: { X1: { Parachain: 2037 } } } })
findAssetInfo('Acala', { location: '{"parents":1,"interior":{"X1":{"Parachain":2037}}}' })

// 2. 'Here' shorthand — DOT on Polkadot, stored location
//    { parents: 1, interior: { Here: null } }
findAssetInfo('Polkadot', { location: { parents: 1, interior: 'Here' } })
findAssetInfo('Polkadot', { location: '{"parents":1,"interior":"Here"}' })

// 3. Reordered keys — ETH on AssetHubPolkadot, stored location
//    { parents: 1, interior: { X3: [{ Parachain: 1000 }, { PalletInstance: 50 }, { GeneralIndex: 20090105 }] } }
findAssetInfo('AssetHubPolkadot', { location: '{"parents":1,"interior":{"X3":[{"Parachain":1000},{"PalletInstance":50},{"GeneralIndex":20090105}]}}' })
findAssetInfo('AssetHubPolkadot', { location: '{"interior":{"X3":[{"Parachain":1000},{"PalletInstance":50},{"GeneralIndex":20090105}]},"parents":1}' })
findAssetInfo('AssetHubPolkadot', { location: { interior: { X3: [{ Parachain: 1000 }, { PalletInstance: 50 }, { GeneralIndex: 20090105 }] }, parents: 1 } })
```

**Observed result (run 2026-11-22, Node.js v24.22.1, Linux, `@paraspell/assets` 14.5.3):**

```
UNQ Acala object V3 X1-object  => UNQ (assetId 10)
UNQ Acala string V3 X1-object  => null
DOT Polkadot object interior "Here" => DOT
DOT Polkadot string interior "Here" => null
ETH AssetHubPolkadot canonical string       => ETH (assetId 20090105)
ETH AssetHubPolkadot reordered-keys string  => null
ETH AssetHubPolkadot reordered-keys object  => ETH (assetId 20090105)
```

The canonical string works (control), so the string branch is not broken outright — it only misses the non-canonical serializations that the object branch handles.

## Environment

- OS: Linux
- Framework: Node.js v24.22.1
- XCM Tool version: @paraspell/assets 14.5.3
- NodeJS version: v24.22.1

## Screenshots / Logs

Live run output, 2026-11-22 (local Node script, no network, no transaction):

```
UNQ Acala object V3 X1-object => UNQ (assetId 10)
UNQ Acala string V3 X1-object => null
DOT Polkadot object interior "Here" => DOT (assetId native)
DOT Polkadot string interior "Here" => null
ETH AssetHubPolkadot canonical string => ETH (assetId 20090105)
ETH AssetHubPolkadot reordered-keys string => null
ETH AssetHubPolkadot reordered-keys object => ETH (assetId 20090105)
```

## Expected Behavior

Each string form returns the same asset as the equivalent object form: UNQ for the V3 string, DOT for the `'Here'` string, ETH for the reordered-keys string.

## Additional Context

Suggested fix: in the string branch, parse and canonicalize before comparing, falling back to the current raw comparison when the string is not JSON:

```ts
if (typeof location === 'string') {
  try {
    const parsed = JSON.parse(location)
    return foreignAssets.find(asset => asset.location && deepEqual(canonicalizeLocation(parsed), canonicalizeLocation(asset.location)))
  } catch {
    return foreignAssets.find(asset => compareLocations(location, asset))
  }
}
```

